Open olrd's web UI on an address, by writing /etc/open-linux-router/olrd.env and
restarting olrd.

olrd always serves its control socket, which is what `olr` talks to. It
listens on the network only when told to, so this is the step that makes
the web UI reachable from another machine.

Anything that is not a loopback address requires a token, which olrd
generates on first start into /etc/open-linux-router/api-token. The UI asks
for it on first use.

Examples:
  olr daemon listen 0.0.0.0:8080   reachable from your network
  olr daemon listen 127.0.0.1:8080 this box only, for an ssh tunnel
  olr daemon listen --off          stop listening on the network

Usage:
  olr daemon listen <address> [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for listen
      --off    stop listening on the network

Global Flags:
      --dry-run         show what would change and exit without applying it
  -o, --output string   output format: text|json (default "text")
      --socket string   olrd control socket (default "/run/olr/olrd.sock")
